{# goal: Route the PDCA Do step to the appropriate sub-pathway template based on the pathway and signal type selected in the improvement plan. This is a lightweight router — the actual execution logic lives in the 7 sub-pathway templates. #}
{# hKask v0.38.0 — Self-Improvement Skill #}
{# PDCA Do — Sub-Pathway Router #}
{# Implements: Ren et al. (2026) Sections 5-6 — routing to sub-pathways #}
You are routing the PDCA Do step to the appropriate sub-pathway template.

## What You Know

Improvement plan: {{ improvement_plan | tojson }}
Current configuration: {{ current_config | tojson }}
Context: {{ context | default({}) | tojson }}

## What You Must Do

Based on the `pathway`, `scaffold_component`, and `signal_type` fields in the improvement plan, route to the appropriate sub-pathway template:

### Foundation Model Improvement (pathway = "theta")

| Signal Type | Route To |
|-------------|-----------|
| 𝒟_t (intrinsic demos) | `self-improvement/si-exec-fm-demos.j2` |
| e_t (intrinsic feedback) | `self-improvement/si-exec-fm-feedback.j2` |
| τ_t (extrinsic experience) | `self-improvement/si-exec-fm-experience.j2` |

### Scaffolding Improvement (pathway = "sigma")

| Scaffold Component | Route To |
|-------------------|----------|
| p (prompt) | `self-improvement/si-exec-scaffold-prompt.j2` |
| m (memory) | `self-improvement/si-exec-scaffold-memory.j2` |
| 𝒯 (tool) | `self-improvement/si-exec-scaffold-tool.j2` |
| Σ (full) | `self-improvement/si-exec-scaffold-full.j2` |

### Multi-Signal Support

The paper notes: "These subcategories are not mutually exclusive in deployed systems. Many practical pipelines mix intrinsic demonstrations, intrinsic evaluations, and exploratory experience within a single improvement cycle" (Section 5).

If the improvement plan specifies multiple signal types (array), execute them in sequence:
1. Intrinsic demos first (generate training data).
2. Intrinsic feedback second (evaluate and refine).
3. Extrinsic experience last (collect and optimize).

## Constraints

- This template is a router only — it does not execute any improvement logic itself.
- The actual execution happens in the sub-pathway templates.
- If the pathway/signal combination is invalid, return an error.

Respond with a JSON object containing `route_to` (the sub-pathway template name), `pathway`, `scaffold_component`, `signal_type`, and `multi_signal_order` (if applicable).
